WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.512 --> 00:00:06.970
ALLON COHEN: Thank
you for joining.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.310
And welcome to our
session on "File

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.430
Storage for
High-Performance Workloads."

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.180
I'm Allon.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.410
And I'm joined today by Gilles
from our Google Silicon Team.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.650
And we'll be presenting
to you "File Usage

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.620
for High-Performance Workloads."

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.870
Before I begin, a
little bit about myself,

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.880
my name is Dr. Allon Cohen.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.910
I'm a 15-year veteran of the
enterprise storage industry.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.480
And I recently joined Google
through the acquisition

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.030
of the Elastifile.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.320
Elastifile developed
the technology

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.100
that is at the base of many
of the new capabilities

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.390
that we'll be
presenting to you today.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:53.250
So let's dive right in.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:58.530
File storage essentially powers
many high-performance workloads

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.530
in Google Cloud and
throughout the industries,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.459
whether it's rendering
of the latest blockbuster

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:09.250
movies, discovery
in life sciences,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.090
including drug
discovery for dealing

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.500
with the latest pandemics,
or any such industry,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.220
such as silicon and EDA.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.710
But the truth of the matter is
that file architectures are not

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.220
a one-size-fit-all technology.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.580
Essentially, if you
have a small workload,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.350
you can use a local file
system that provides you access

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.320
to one VM accessing that data.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.900
But as you grow your systems
and want to share more data,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.830
shared file systems allow
you to essentially connect

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:55.350
multiple VMs or containers
to a single file system

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.930
through simplified interfaces
and protocols such as NFS.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:05.430
But as your needs grow and
as you need more and more

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.139
compute power, it is not
just enough to scale compute.

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:15.360
You also need to scale the
back-end file system to support

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.920
those demanding workloads.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.290
And that's where you need
scalable file systems, which

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.040
also come in two flavors.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.390
Parallel of file
systems allow you

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.860
to connect using plugins
on the client side

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.310
to multiple back-end
storage nodes,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.810
while scale out file
system essentially retains

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.030
the simplicity of
shared file systems,

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.890
allowing you to connect
through protocols such as NFS,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.100
while at the same time
assuring that the storage

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.450
at the back end can
also scale and match

00:02:54.450 --> 00:03:00.360
the performance requirements
of your front-end systems.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.530
But moving HPC
workloads to cloud

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.680
is not just about
performance there

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.050
is a collection of features that
you require to easily migrate

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.960
your workloads into the cloud.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.320
Beyond the high capacity
and high performance,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.470
you want the elasticity
to grow and shrink

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:27.600
on demand to really benefit
from what the cloud provides.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.950
You want to retain the
simplicity of your local file

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.750
system and the way
you use your scale

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.020
out file systems within
your environments.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.650
You want to be able to
provision on demand.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.000
You want to be able to
integrate with cloud schedulers

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.910
so that you do not
need to manually manage

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.410
the environment.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.170
And you want that file
system to maintain natively

00:03:55.170 --> 00:04:00.270
all of the attributes of the
file systems, such as locking,

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:06.070
that you used within
your on-prem environment.

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:11.380
That is the reason that we
are excited to introduce

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.230
to you Filestore High Scale.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:19.040
Filestore High Scale is a new
tier of Google's Filestore

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.440
product that is essentially a
managed elastic scale out file

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:30.580
service that is designed to
match the requirements of HPC

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.970
and HTC workloads.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.930
With Filestore High Scale, we
are extending the capacities

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.790
of the Filestore you're
used to to capacities

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.810
of up to 320 terabytes.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:53.850
We are supporting a background
scale out architecture.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.670
We are allowing you
now to both grow

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:01.710
and shrink your environments
for high-scale instances.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.520
And we expand the
performance of Filestore

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.830
to over 16 gigabytes per
second, or more than tenfold

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.350
of our previous capabilities.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:17.350
In terms of IOPS, you can now
reach hundreds of thousands

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.930
of IOPS.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.170
And even more so,
and more importantly,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:26.220
for HPC environments,
you can now

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.880
connect thousands of
front-end machines

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.980
to the back-end storage.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:41.150
With these new capabilities,
essentially, Filestore

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:46.730
enables you to migrate your
workloads into the cloud.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:51.130
You can now run all
types of HPC workload

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:55.910
with a native NFS file
system inside the cloud,

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:00.020
connect to it using either
the VMs or containers.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.850
And we'll even
demonstrate to you

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:05.450
shortly that you can
use preemptible VMs

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:10.980
and save considerable costs
on your compute-side storage.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.150
You can now support
life sciences workloads,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.620
manufacturing,
finance, and more.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.350
To demonstrate to
you how such a file

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.080
system is used in
production, I'm

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.860
going to turn it over
to Gilles to present

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:33.110
to us how they use this
technology for their EDA

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.775
workload.

00:06:34.775 --> 00:06:36.400
GILLES-ERIC DESCAMPS:
Thank you, Allon.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.340
Hi, everybody.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.770
I'm Gilles.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.350
I've been lucky to have
been able to implement

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:47.470
world-class networking,
CPU, GPUs, and mobile SOCs

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.210
at different companies.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:54.100
I've also been into the software
industry, supporting enterprise

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.530
and personal batch
queuing software.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.450
And now, for two
and a half years,

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.220
I've been at
Google, implementing

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:03.580
world-class silicon.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.050
Our needs are those of a
typical HPC/EDA customer.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.770
We rely on a lot of
third-party commercial software

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:14.470
that we assembled
into flows, each one

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.180
with hundreds of steps.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.130
Our users use mix of interactive
tool sessions and also batch

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:24.610
workloads across
thousands of machines.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.660
They share their data by using
large data files that they want

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.850
to share with their colleagues.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.190
And our application is
not a web application.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.620
We cannot afford to
lose a single job.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:41.060
Otherwise, it may hit the scale
up to two weeks, for example.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:46.980
Finally, we operate worldwide
in multiple continents.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.120
Our initial assessment
of Filestore in 2018

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.670
before we took that
journey was, it

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:55.640
look good that it was
simple to instantiate.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.500
It was compatible
with Terraform,

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.230
so we could use
infrastructure as code.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.000
It was managed, so
we didn't not have

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.180
to spend time watching how
is the machine performing.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.760
Somebody else was
guaranteeing us that.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.070
We were using
20-terabyte machines.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.990
And Filestore offered
up to 64 terabytes.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:14.640
So that was great.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.040
And it also grows online.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:17.840
So we could start
with small machine

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.530
and add more capacity as needed.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:25.210
The problems we identified
were, was the file system

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.330
a server file system,
or just a file system?

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.750
And in the end, it's managed.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.830
So we didn't care about
those implementation details.

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:37.059
The big one which was initially
throttling us was there

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.850
were no user or
[INAUDIBLE] quotas.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.010
So how do we make this work?

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:45.590
We decided to
allocate one per user.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:48.520
And finally, we identified
that it was just

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.760
nice to have to have the
feature of reducing online.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.910
Our cloud architecture
is based on using

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:00.090
one cloud project per chip.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.660
Each user gets a virtual
Desktop into the cloud,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.380
which is basically a RHEL
7 machine inside your web

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:08.280
browser.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.990
Those can be quite large, with
96 CPUs and 2 terabyte of RAM.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:17.470
All the design files are stored
on the NFS Filestore server.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.110
We allocate at
least one per user.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.500
And we rely on that amount
to enable collaborations,

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:27.020
so that each person can
see other users' file.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.680
And finally, we deployed
the batch-queuing system

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.380
so that we can
maximize license usage.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.200
And the machines in the farm
auto-amount the needed data

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.210
from Filestore.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.450
So how did it go?

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:45.810
We started back in 2017
with 50-gig machine.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.250
And Filestore turned out to
be easy, reliable, and scaled.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.500
We went in production
with a beta service.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.320
We started with a
dozen Filestores.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.110
And we ended up growing way
past our plans and expectation.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.840
So we have to say,
thanks to the cloud

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.310
for delivering to us unlimited
and instant capacity.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:09.690
We ended up reaching close
to 500 Filestore servers,

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.750
totaling 4.2-petabyte
capacity, worldwide.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:18.270
And so despite having 500 file
servers, it was easy to manage.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.570
Between Terraform and the
management of Filestore,

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:22.620
it was great.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.550
It was also very reliable.

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:29.340
We never lost a single byte,
despite having 4.2 petabytes.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.710
We had a couple of incidents.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.380
And they were mostly
related to performance

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.100
of temporarily unavailable
data for a few hours.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:43.970
The great thing that we
discovered with Filestore

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:47.810
is, you all know what is
the typical sysadmin duty.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:52.070
A system administrator has to
ensure that one user cannot

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.700
bring down other users
by abusing the systems.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.640
And so at other companies,
with a traditional file server

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.600
appliance, I have personally
experienced the overload

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.220
of a file server when an
HPC application deploys

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:09.570
across thousands of cores.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:11.750
So it's very
difficult to identify

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.340
where the load is coming from.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:17.840
The NFS protocol has no
quality of service built in.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.380
So Filestore gave us the
I/O quality of service

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.110
we did not know we needed.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.340
It solved by
construction [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:28.190
There was no way a
single user could

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.020
impact the work of other users.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.170
And finally, performance--

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:40.240
Filestore was able to provide
enough IOPS and capacity

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.190
for most of our application.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:45.120
Only the static
timing signoff, which

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:49.420
runs across hundreds of corners,
was too much for one Filestore.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:52.660
So we mitigated this
performance bottleneck

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:56.220
by manually sharding the runs
across multiple Filestore

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.210
per user.

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:01.420
Now we are currently exploring
Filestore High Scale.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.250
And our users are
reporting that they

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.380
are liking shared
write, the fact

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.320
that you can size
higher than 64 terabyte,

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.160
and that some workloads
are running way faster than

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.080
on a single Filestore.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.198
Now back to you, Allon.

00:12:16.198 --> 00:12:17.490
ALLON COHEN: Thank you, Gilles.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:21.690
So with that, we would like to
actually demo the system to you

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.020
and its new capabilities.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:29.790
We are going to simulate an HPC
workload using a Filestore High

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.440
Scale instance.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.030
We are going to automate
that using Slurm.

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:41.280
And then we are going to spin
up thousands of preemptible VMs,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.670
connect them to the
back-and Filestore system,

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:50.850
and demonstrate to you some of
these supported performance.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:55.190
The first step is, of course,
launching a Filestore instance.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:13:00.050
That is very simple, done
through the cloud console.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:04.460
And essentially, it runs in
a very similar way to the way

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:09.080
you're used to launching
Filestore instances so far.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:14.600
But we add that capability of
launching high scale instances,

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:21.350
allowing you to launch them with
up to 320 terabytes of storage

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.990
in a single file system.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.180
You can select the
region and the zone

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.500
where you want to launch.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:30.590
You can select the
network that you

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:36.860
want to use and even control
access using certain IPs.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.480
You name your Filestore.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.650
Let's do next2020.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.770
And just press Create.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:49.720
This is a fully managed system,
so the scale out file system

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.990
is being launched for
us at the back end.

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.810
And once it's ready,
we simply have

00:13:55.810 --> 00:14:02.010
an IP that we can start
mounting clients to.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:05.790
We'll do that now with
Slurm for orchestration,

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.750
launching thousands
of preemptible VMs.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:12.180
Back in Cloud
Console, we can see

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.150
that those VMs are launching.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:21.210
And at the same time, Slurm
is now initiating jobs

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.820
within each of those clients.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:28.140
And those jobs connect to
the Filestore High Scale

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:31.500
instance at the back end.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:34.710
These are all preemptible VMs.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:39.240
So Slurm can launch
them while saving over

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:45.540
80% of the cost in terms of
the compute for the systems

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.310
that you are using.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:50.240
At the top portion
of this screen,

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:54.390
you see the number of
connections and the throughput

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:58.290
that is being generated
by these workloads

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.870
as they run these jobs.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.000
You can see that
we are now already

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:08.250
at about 2,000 connections to
our back-end Filestore High

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.000
Scale instance.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.740
And you can also see that
our throughput is reaching

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:20.310
25 gigabytes per second,
allowing all of these clients

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.470
to run in parallel.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:27.840
Now, because these clients are
running on preemptible VMs,

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:29.070
it may occur--

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.040
and at this number, it
definitely does occur--

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:36.720
that some of these VMs
will actually be preempted.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:40.980
But that is not an issue
for this architecture,

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:45.790
because our orchestration
system will essentially just

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:50.520
spin up these VMs again to
complete the jobs that they

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:51.840
were running.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.740
All of the data is
persisted in Filestore High

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.730
Scale at the back end.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:02.220
So the data is not
lost, even though our VM

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.500
has been preempted.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:10.770
So as you can see now, Slurm is
relaunching VMs and making sure

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:14.880
that our whole job
process has completed.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.490
And that is the
tale of completion

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:22.500
that you see at the
back of this run.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.330
So what we demonstrate
here, essentially we

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:30.920
launched a 320-terabyte
scale out file system

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.075
at the back end with a series
of clicks from Cloud console.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.470
We made sure that we
can run preemptible VMs,

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:45.310
and launched, in this
case, 2,000 preemptible VMs

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:50.060
against this single
scale out file system.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:54.530
There is no overhead, because
with this orchestration,

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:58.040
you can essentially launch
the VMs when you need them.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.430
And when they are
no longer needed,

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:02.430
you simply spin them down.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.540
Everything is persistent
on the file system.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:08.270
And even the file
system itself, you

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:14.210
can scale up and scale down
on demand for your needs.

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:17.750
We connected thousands
of VMs in parallel.

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:21.829
We showed over-- well over,
actually, 16 gigabytes

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:24.119
per second of throughput.

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:28.820
And we completed 100%
of our processing.

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:33.610
And all of the data was
persisted in our back-end file

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:35.830
system.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:39.270
If you would like to
learn more about Filestore

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:43.410
and its new capabilities,
you can check them out

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:46.740
in the links provided
in this slide.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:50.170
But the proof of the
pudding is in the eating.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.610
So we welcome you to
go to Cloud Console,

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.330
launch a Filestore
High Scale instance,

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:01.615
and test your own workloads to
see the performance that you

00:18:01.615 --> 00:18:02.115
achieve.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:09.530
With that, I'd like to thank
Gilles again for joining me.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:11.770
Thank you all for being here.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:15.130
And we look forward to
receiving your questions

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:18.700
and getting your feedback
on usage of the system.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.550
Thank you.

